# Hydrotonic No-Wave

**Ingredients:** INDIAN JAL TARANG + NO WAVE / ANGULAR POST-PUNK + PREPARED BARITONE GUITAR + MRIDANGAM + CONTRABASSOON + SHORT ANALOG BASS

**Creative Catalyst:** The genre began during a monsoon-season residency inside a decommissioned municipal water-testing laboratory in Pune, where jal tarang player Ananya Deshmukh was calibrating porcelain bowls by changing their water levels while New York guitarist Mara Keene rehearsed abrasive no-wave figures in the adjacent pump room. The useful connection appeared when Ananya deliberately retuned one bowl between repetitions instead of restoring it to concert pitch. Mara stopped treating the pitch drift as a mistake and changed one guitar interval to follow it. By the fourth cycle, the harmony had migrated several cents without any conventional chord change. Mridangam, contrabassoon, prepared baritone guitar, and a short analog bass voice were added to create a form where live retuning itself becomes the harmonic engine.

Hydrotonic No-Wave is an acoustic-electric art-rock form built around unstable tuning rather than unstable tempo. No wave supplies the attitude and architecture: dry drums, asymmetric riffs, abrasive intervals, clipped repetition, anti-solo guitar, sudden ensemble stops, and harmony that often feels structural rather than expressive. Jal tarang supplies a completely different logic: rows of water-tuned porcelain bowls whose pitch can be altered physically by adding or removing small amounts of water.

The jal tarang is recorded with a close stereo pair above the bowl arc, one low microphone near the table surface to capture body resonance and mallet impact, and a restrained room pair. The close pair preserves pitch separation and the glassy ceramic attack. The low microphone captures water movement, bowl body, table vibration, and the slight splash or finger contact produced during retuning. The room pair remains narrow and dry enough that each bowl still feels like an individual tuned object rather than a shimmering ambient cloud.

Players favor two- to five-note cells, repeated dyads, open fifths, minor seconds, tritones, octave displacement, sparse arpeggio fragments, single-note pulses, and deliberately exposed unisons between adjacent bowls. Virtuosic cascading runs are possible but used rarely. The core sound comes from hearing a familiar pitch return slightly altered after the water level changes.

The central compositional device is the **Waterline Ledger**. Every composition begins with a written row of bowl pitches and a second row showing planned water changes in milliliters or relative marks. Retuning occurs during the piece, not only between songs. One bowl may drop slightly while its neighbor remains fixed; another may rise toward a dissonant unison; a three-note figure may become progressively narrower until two notes nearly beat against each other. The ensemble follows the resulting pitch field by ear and through simple written checkpoints rather than forcing the jal tarang back to equal temperament.

Unlike the recent acoustic-electronic systems built around attack states or triggering events, Hydrotonic No-Wave does not assign one instrument to react automatically to another. The band instead shares a moving tuning environment. A baritone-guitar chord that was consonant in the first section may become dissonant in the second because one bowl has shifted. The composition develops through that changing relationship.

Prepared baritone guitar provides the principal abrasive countervoice. Flatwound or heavy roundwound strings are used with partial palm muting, behind-the-bridge plucks, alligator-clip or removable paper preparations, deadened harmonics, short feedback bursts, and dry chord stabs. Chords favor fourths, tritones, minor seconds, open strings, and two- or three-note voicings. Continuous distortion walls are avoided; the guitar should remain dry enough that pitch relationships with the bowls stay obvious.

The guitar is often retuned only partially. One or two strings may remain fixed while another is adjusted by ear to a drifting jal tarang pitch. This creates split tuning systems inside one instrument. Alternate tunings are treated as compositional tools, not as exotic color.

Mridangam supplies the main pulse but is stripped of ceremonial framing. Dry strokes, muted bass-head pressure, sharp treble taps, gumki bends, fingertip rolls, and single-korvai fragments are used as angular rhythmic punctuation. The player avoids continuous classical accompaniment. Patterns often repeat unevenly across 4/4, 5/4, 7/8, and additive bars, giving the music forward motion without turning it into progressive-rock display.

Contrabassoon occupies the lowest acoustic register with dry fundamentals, rough reed starts, short chromatic falls, multiphonic edges, key noise, and heavy pedal tones. It rarely plays flowing lines. Its role is to make the changing tuning field feel physically large: when a jal tarang bowl drifts downward, the contrabassoon may hold the old pitch so the beating relationship remains exposed.

Short analog bass is used sparingly. It is monophonic, tightly enveloped, and usually follows fixed reference pitches rather than the drifting bowls. This creates a stable electrical ruler against which acoustic intonation can move. Long sub tails and club-style bass drops are excluded.

Percussion outside the mridangam remains minimal. A compact dry drum kit may add kick, floor tom, snare rim, and one dead cymbal, but cymbal wash is discouraged. Found laboratory sounds may appear as real-scale punctuation: pipette taps, glass stirring rods, valve handles, porcelain lid clicks, measuring-cylinder contact, and water poured into enamel trays. They remain recognizable and brief.

Vocals are optional and anti-melodic. Short spoken lines, shouted inventory numbers, counting fragments, sustained single vowels, or dry unison speech may appear, but conventional verse-chorus singing is not required. When pitch is used vocally, singers are encouraged to hold a fixed note while the jal tarang drifts around it.

The defining tension is calibration versus refusal. Every object in the room suggests measurement and exactness, yet the music deliberately preserves tuning states that a conventional engineer or technician would correct.

## Genre DNA

**Tempo & Pulse:** 92–126 BPM, with dry 4/4, 5/4, 7/8, and mixed additive bars. Repetition is essential, but phrase lengths are often 3, 5, or 7 bars rather than standard 4- or 8-bar blocks. Mridangam and dry kit emphasize contour rather than virtuosity.

**Harmonic Palette:** Dorian, Aeolian, Bhairavi-adjacent interval colors used non-dogmatically, open fifths, tritones, minor seconds, octave pedals, unstable unisons, split-tuned guitar strings, and slowly shifting microtonal relationships created by water-level retuning.

**Production Signatures:**

1. **Waterline Ledger** — jal tarang bowls are retuned during performance according to planned water-level changes, creating evolving pitch relationships rather than fixed harmony.
2. **Old-Pitch Witness** — contrabassoon, analog bass, voice, or one fixed guitar string may hold the previous pitch after a bowl moves, exposing audible beating and drift.
3. **Split-Tuning Guitar** — only selected baritone-guitar strings follow the moving bowl pitches while others remain fixed.
4. **Dry Ceramic Authority** — the main jal tarang attack stays close and unwashed; long shimmer reverbs are excluded from the core mix.
5. **Measured Pour** — audible water additions or removals may remain in the arrangement as literal retuning events, but they are not rhythmically exaggerated into foley spectacle.
6. **No-Resolution Obligation** — if a section ends in a beating near-unison or abrasive interval, the next section does not have to correct it. Drift may remain unresolved for the rest of the track.

**Dynamics:** Intensity grows through narrower pitch spacing, louder ceramic attack, stronger mridangam bass bends, harsher prepared-guitar articulation, denser mixed-meter repetition, and more forceful contrabassoon pedals. Climaxes remain dry, confrontational, and physically small-scale rather than cinematic.

## Scene & Origin Lore

Hydrotonic No-Wave formed inside a retired municipal water-quality and hydraulic-calibration laboratory on the industrial edge of Pune. The building retained white ceramic sinks, stainless benches, faded blue pipe labels, burettes, enamel trays, analog pressure gauges, numbered sample racks, cracked terrazzo floors, glass cabinets, and long drainage channels cut into the laboratory floor.

Jal tarang player **Ananya Deshmukh** had been invited to document historical tuning methods and compare different porcelain bowl sets. She became fascinated by how little water was required to move a pitch enough for two adjacent bowls to produce slow beating. Instead of treating those differences as setup errors, she began writing down the amount of drift itself.

Guitarist-composer **Mara Keene** was in India through an experimental-music exchange centered on repetition and material acoustics. Her no-wave background favored short dissonant cells and deliberately unpolished tone. During the first rehearsal she kept one baritone-guitar dyad fixed while Ananya lowered one bowl by several small pours. The guitar never changed, but the perceived harmony transformed each time the ceramic pitch moved.

Mridangam player **Raghav Iyer** joined after hearing that the project needed pulse without harmonic smoothing. He reduced his setup to a close, dry sound and built short asymmetrical figures around the changing phrase lengths. Contrabassoonist **Elsa Varga** added fixed low pedals that made microtonal drift easier to perceive. Electronics musician **Dev Malhotra** completed the group with a compact analog bass synth and laboratory-object recordings used only as sparse reference sounds.

Their first performances were called **calibration failures**. Before each piece, the audience could see the starting water levels marked on narrow paper strips beside the bowls. During the piece Ananya changed selected levels with laboratory droppers and small measuring cups. The point was not theatrical precision; the visible measurements made it clear that the pitch instability was intentional.

The scene developed a visual language of porcelain white, faded laboratory blue, oxidized steel, mineral green, rust-red gauge needles, graphite black, wet concrete grey, and dark indigo clothing. The unofficial rule is that any tuning discrepancy interesting enough to hear is too valuable to correct immediately.

