# Fictional EP Tracklist

## 1. Bowl Nine Drops Three Millimeters

**Story & Context:** The debut runs at 108 BPM in a repeating five-bar phrase. Ananya begins with four jal tarang pitches forming an open fifth plus two closely spaced upper notes. Mara plays a fixed baritone-guitar dyad beneath them while Raghav repeats a dry mridangam pattern that resolves every five bars.

After the third cycle, Ananya adds a measured amount of water to bowl nine. Its pitch drops slightly, turning what had been a clean upper interval into a slow beating near-unison. Mara does not retune. Elsa enters on the old pitch with a low contrabassoon harmonic, making the difference between original and new tuning explicit.

Dev contributes one short analog reference tone every ten bars, always unchanged. The track becomes more dissonant even though the electronic note never moves. In the final section Ananya lowers bowl nine again, but this time Mara adjusts only one guitar string toward the new pitch. The other string remains fixed, creating a split chord whose beating continues after the ensemble stops.

**Visual & Lyrics Prompt:** Close overhead image of nine porcelain bowls on a stainless laboratory bench, bowl 9 marked with a blue measurement strip showing a three-millimeter water-level change, baritone-guitar string crossing the frame and an analog pressure gauge nearby. No conventional lyrics; sparse spoken fragments limited to “nine,” “three millimeters,” “leave the old pitch,” and “do not correct it.”

## 2. Reference Tone Refuses the New Waterline

**Story & Context:** At 122 BPM, Dev opens with a single fixed analog note pulsing every two bars. Raghav builds a clipped 7/8 mridangam figure while Mara plays two dry prepared-guitar stabs per cycle. Ananya begins with three bowls tuned close to the analog reference.

Every eight cycles, she removes a small amount of water from one bowl, raising its pitch. The analog synth never follows. By the midpoint the original near-unison has become an obvious minor-second conflict. Elsa alternates between matching the fixed reference and matching the drifting bowl, making the listener hear both tuning systems as equally valid.

Mara eventually retunes one guitar string by ear toward the raised bowl but leaves the other strings unchanged. The result is not a modulation; the ensemble now contains three simultaneous standards of pitch. The final minute removes drums entirely. Ceramic strikes, fixed analog tone, split-tuned guitar, and contrabassoon remain exposed until Ananya stops changing the bowl.

**Visual & Lyrics Prompt:** Laboratory reference oscillator beside a porcelain jal tarang bowl whose waterline has been raised above a marked calibration stripe, three diverging pitch lines drawn on white inspection paper. Spoken fragments limited to “reference stays,” “water goes higher,” “three standards,” and “none of them wins.”

## 3. Drain Channel Keeps the Wrong Unison

**Story & Context:** The closer begins at 96 BPM with no analog bass. Ananya tunes two bowls into an intentionally rough near-unison and repeats them in alternation. Mara plays one heavily muted baritone-guitar note between every ceramic strike. Raghav uses only low mridangam pressure bends and fingertip taps.

Elsa holds one long contrabassoon pitch equidistant from the two bowls, producing a dense acoustic beating field without any electronic modulation. Every sixteen bars Ananya changes the water in only one bowl by a very small amount. Sometimes the beating slows; sometimes it accelerates. The composition follows the rate of beating rather than a conventional chord sequence.

In the last section, Dev introduces one short synth tone exactly at the original starting pitch, revealing how far both bowls have drifted. Mara switches off distortion completely and plays the old pitch as a natural harmonic. No one restores the jal tarang. The final sound is water being poured from one bowl into the laboratory drain channel while the two changed pitches remain the last remembered harmony.

